. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. ********************** Testing H3: #ManyDaughters Project *******************
> ***
. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. 
. * Authors: [REDACTED], [REDACTED]
. 
. *------------------------------------------------------------------------*
. * H3 : Having daughters leads to more gender equality in the household
. * (as measured by behaviors or outcomes).
. *------------------------------------------------------------------------*
.  
. /*
>          Assumptions
>         *----------------------------
>         
>         Same as in H1:
>             -- Child's gender is randomly assigned. 
>                 -- Parents are a selected subsample (child of any gender can 
> affect political preferences)
>         
>         From this assumptions we arrive to similar conclusions concerning the
>  relevant 
>         comparison and treatment groups. 
>         
>     There is an additional assumption here. More equal households are those w
> here
>         men and women have a similar distribution of tasks. The amount of hou
> rs worked in 
>         childcare is similar, the amount of time worked for pay is similar, e
> tc. This 
>         does not have to be the case. Households where members specialize in 
> different
>         domains (housework, paid employment) are not egalitarian, even if thi
> s specialization
>         reflects only economic considerations. 
>         
>                         
>            Implementation
>          *------------------------------ 
>         
>         The unit of analysis for this question is the household, as we consid
> er how
>         different is the task allocation between men and women. We measure th
> e differences
>         using Duncan segregation index. While it was originally intended to m
> easure
>         occupational segregation by gender, race, etc, it can be adapted for 
> the analysis
>         of household tasks. In our implementation, we consider the difference
> s in the 
>         fraction of time that men and women allocate to tasks. Higher numbers
>  show greater 
>         differences. 
>         
>         The list of tasks considered in the analysis are: 
>                 * pli0038_h - Job, apprenticeship, second job (including trav
> el time to and from work)
>                 * pli0040 - Errands (shopping, trips to government agencies, 
> etc.)
>                 * pli0043_v3 - Housework (washing, cooking, cleaning)
>                 * pli0044_v3 - Childcare
>                 * pli0046 - Care and support of persons in need of care
>                 * pli0047_v1 - Education or further training (also school, un
> iversity)
>                 * pli0049_v3 - Repairs on and around the house, car repairs, 
> garden work
>                 * pli0051 - Hobbies and other free-time activities
> 
> 
>         The implementation requires additional steps, when compared to other 
> hypothesis, 
>         as we need to first match individuals and create households. Second, 
> we focus on 
>         children born to a couple, i.e. we do not consider patchwork families
> . Third, 
>         we need verify that information on tasks is completed (not only missi
> ng values)
>         and that respondents record at least one task.
>         
>         We include only households that meet the following criteria
>                 - They do not have children at the time of their  first obser
> vation 
>                 - They will eventually have children
>                 - They are heterosexual couples
>                 - In households where there are more than two couples, we kee
> p the youngest couple 
>                 - We exclude households with patchwork families (children fro
> m previous couples)
>         
>         We estimate the effects of parenting a daughter using a diff-in-diff 
> approach. 
>         Following the new litereature on the topic, we first estimate a regre
> ssion that 
>         includes many (many) interactions, to avoid forbidden comparisons. We
>  obtain
>         estimates for every cohort, and every time since the event. Finally, 
> we average
>         the results to obtain a single coefficient. 
>                 */
. 
